# Why each issue matters: who benefits and what changes for them (English; paste-ready).
BENEFIT = {
"A1": "The team keeps shipping 2.x fixes after 3.0 work starts, and new packages publish without hand-edited lists. Without it, the first 3.0 tag blocks every 2.x release.",
"T1": "Apps install and ship only the chains they use. An EVM-only app drops about 83% of the SDK bundle and goes from 777 MB to about 85 MB on install (35 → 0 advisories). Adding a chain touches one plugin instead of files spread across 9 packages (Stacks appears in 67 files today).",
"T2": "Tree-shaking finally works: importing one helper costs under 1 KB instead of 832 KB gzip. Every later size win builds on it.",
"T3": "Apps pick wallets per chain (`adapters={[evm(), solana()]}`) and load only those; the wallet layer is 2.6 MB gzip today, the EVM part alone 124 KB. Per-provider state makes SSR, tests and multiple providers safe.",
"T4": "Apps share one viem, wagmi and react-query copy with the SDK instead of up to 8 viem copies (355 MB), and can pick up security fixes without waiting for a SODAX release (e.g. the `ws` advisory in the pinned viem).",
"T5": "Every install loses 131 MB, 20 advisories and six nested viem copies, which even apps that never touch the DEX pay today. The math stays identical, checked by property tests.",
"T6": "Browser bundles stop carrying private-key signing code and its libraries (near-api-js, ecpair, secp256k1, bip322-js), so there is less attack surface in the browser. Backends get one clear server entry.",
"T7": "The WalletConnect, MetaMask and Coinbase SDKs become optional. The wagmi stack drops from 576 MB / 23 advisories to 102 MB / 0, the deprecated MetaMask SDK goes away, and apps stay on a supported wagmi.",
"T8": "ICON, Stellar and Solana users get smaller installs and fewer network calls (Solana: 2 RPCs fewer per deposit, 1 per message). It also fixes ICON receipts that are typed `bigint` but are BigNumber at runtime.",
"T9": "Fixes a v2 bug. Under `SodaxWalletProvider`, dapp-kit hooks silently use wagmi's private QueryClient, so app settings such as a Sentry `onError` or a cache reset never apply.",
"T10": "The token list stops being copied into four packages (a single swap dapp carries five copies today). Bundles shrink, and chain and token data have one source of truth.",
"T11": "Locks in every gain: a PR that grows the bundle or the install footprint, or adds advisories, fails CI, and every change shows before/after numbers. Without it, v3 savings regress quietly.",
"T12": "Apps import only the features they use (`@sodax/sdk/swap`). An EVM swap-only app is about 169 KB gzip, against 1.27 MB for `new Sodax()` today, and the familiar `Sodax` class keeps working.",
"T13": "React apps load only the hook groups they use, and no longer get the whole SDK through dapp-kit's re-export; Next.js apps get correct `'use client'` boundaries.",
"T14": "Code that only needs chain keys and helpers (backends, light clients, the API packages) stops paying for the token list: `getChainType` goes from 34 KB to 2 KB gzip. Non-breaking.",
"T15": "The SDK core runs in Cloudflare Workers, React Native, Bun and Deno without any Buffer or crypto polyfill setup.",
"T16": "AI agents and the skills CLI write correct v3 code, while 2.x users keep getting 2.x guidance instead of a mix.",
"T17": "Integrators upgrade by running a codemod instead of reading a diff. In-repo apps migrate early, so breaking PRs stay small, and sodax-frontend, sodax-backend and intents-whitelabel each get a tracked upgrade.",
"T18": "Money market, staking, migration and DEX flows get the same progress states as swap, bridge and leverage-yield, and recover after a page reload or a relay timeout.",
"T19": "Apps can tell retryable API errors from fatal ones and cancel stale quotes and polls, which saves requests. Support can follow one user action from the SDK into backend logs by request id.",
"T20": "New tokens and chains go live without an SDK release, and token lists leave the bundle, if product approves the trust model.",
"T21": "Apps can prefetch, server-render and suspend on SODAX data, and invalidate caches reliably with exported keys. The stale-allowance key bug is fixed along the way.",
"T22": "Less boilerplate: hooks find the connected wallet themselves instead of every call passing `walletProvider` (55 hook files take it today), and apps handle one error type everywhere.",
"T23": "Custom wallets (MPC, Safe, embedded) implement a few sign/send methods instead of RPC reads. One ICONEX queue restores the WALLET-L-1 audit fix across modules.",
"T24": "Partners pick the right integration (SDK, API mode or raw transactions) and install exactly the packages it needs.",
"T25": "Bitcoin raw transactions from the swaps and leverage-yield APIs parse correctly. Today `value` stays a string although it is typed `bigint`.",
"T26": "Predictable hook and parameter names make the API easier to learn and easier for AI agents to use. App and provider share one SDK instance (no second `new Sodax()`).",
"T27": "Docs and AI tools serve the released version, not `main`, so 2.x users never get v3 instructions during the breaking window. The generated indexes stop drifting.",
"T28": "One light package for every SODAX HTTP API, with one transport and one error type, usable without the SDK. The duplicated HTTP stacks go away. Proposed: API-only React apps get hooks from `@sodax/api/react` and stop installing the SDK (21 MB instead of ≥ 777 MB).",
"T29": "CI proves that the SDK works on Bun, Deno and Workers, so runtime support is a tested promise.",
"T30": "Chain-specific account data comes with correct types, so apps need fewer casts.",
"T31": "One wallet prompt (approve + action) for the remaining features; typed test fakes for integrators; a lighter, maintained Solana stack (web3.js v1 receives critical fixes only).",
"T32": "Apps that already use a raw EIP-1193 provider, a viem WalletClient or Wallet Standard plug in without wagmi or SODAX's React layer.",
"T33": "AI agents build SODAX transactions through the existing MCP servers from one schema source, and never hold keys.",
"T34": "Backend API changes that would silently break client parsing get caught before users hit them.",
"T35": "Polling load is tuned in one place, and quote, swap and status hooks can run over the API with the same hook API.",
"T36": "Embedded and social logins beyond Privy plug in the same way, each as an optional subpath.",
}
